"[2510.13842v1] ADMIT: Few-shot Knowledge Poisoning Attacks on RAG-based Fact Checking (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13842v1)\nciteturn2741view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13842v1\",\"lineno\":14}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 11 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 15 May 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:ADMIT: Few-shot Knowledge Poisoning Attacks on RAG-based Fact Checking\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Yutao Wu , cite7†Xiao Liu , cite8†Yinghui Li , cite9†Yifeng Gao , cite10†Yifan Ding , cite11†Jiale Ding , cite12†Xiang Zheng , cite13†Xingjun Ma L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled ADMIT: Few-shot Knowledge Poisoning Attacks on RAG-based Fact Checking, by Yutao Wu and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite14†View PDF cite15†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Knowledge poisoning poses a critical threat to Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems by injecting adversarial content into knowledge bases, tricking Large Language Models (LLMs) into producing attacker-controlled outputs grounded in manipulated context. Prior work highlights LLMs' susceptibility to misleading or malicious retrieved content. However, real-world fact-checking scenarios are more challenging, as credible evidence typically dominates the retrieval pool.\nL17: To investigate this problem, we extend knowledge poisoning to the fact-checking setting, where retrieved context includes authentic supporting or refuting evidence. We propose \\textbf{ADMIT} (\\textbf{AD}versarial \\textbf{M}ulti-\\textbf{I}njection \\textbf{T}echnique), a few-shot, semantically aligned poisoning attack that flips fact-checking decisions and induces deceptive justifications, all without access to the target LLMs, retrievers, or token-level control.\nL18: Extensive experiments show that ADMIT transfers effectively across 4 retrievers, 11 LLMs, and 4 cross-domain benchmarks, achieving an average attack success rate (ASR) of 86\\% at an extremely low poisoning rate of $0.93 \\times 10^{-6}$, and remaining robust even in the presence of strong counter-evidence. Compared with prior state-of-the-art attacks, ADMIT improves ASR by 11.2\\% across all settings, exposing significant vulnerabilities in real-world RAG-based fact-checking systems.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2510.13842 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2510.13842v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.13842†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Oscar Wu [cite19†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Sat, 11 Oct 2025 14:50:40 UTC (784 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v2] Fri, 15 May 2026 11:32:43 UTC (773 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled ADMIT: Few-shot Knowledge Poisoning Attacks on RAG-based Fact Checking, by Yutao Wu and 7 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite14†View PDF L36:   * cite15†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite20†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite21†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L45: \nL46: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2025-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite27†cs L51: cite28†cs.AI L52: cite29†cs.CR L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite30†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite31†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite32†Semanti