apers\nL133: # Recommenders and Search Tools\nL134: \nL135: [Input] Link to Influence Flower\nL136: \nL137: Influence Flower (cite46†What are Influence Flowers?†influencemap.cmlab.dev )\nL138: \nL139: [Input] Core recommender toggle\nL140: \nL141: CORE Recommender (cite47†What is CORE?†core.ac.uk )\nL142: \nL143:   * Author\nL144:   * Venue\nL145:   * Institution\nL146:   * Topic\nL147: \nL148: [Input] About arXivLabs\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.11108v1] A Vision for Access Control in LLM-based Agent Systems (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11108v1)\nciteturn2741view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11108v1\",\"lineno\":14}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Multiagent Systems\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 13 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 19 Oct 2025 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:A Vision for Access Control in LLM-based Agent Systems\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Xinfeng Li , cite7†Dong Huang , cite8†Jie Li , cite9†Hongyi Cai , cite10†Zhenhong Zhou , cite11†Wei Dong , cite12†XiaoFeng Wang , cite13†Yang Liu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled A Vision for Access Control in LLM-based Agent Systems, by Xinfeng Li and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite14†View PDF cite15†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:The autonomy and contextual complexity of LLM-based agents render traditional access control (AC) mechanisms insufficient. Static, rule-based systems designed for predictable environments are fundamentally ill-equipped to manage the dynamic information flows inherent in agentic interactions.\nL17: This position paper argues for a paradigm shift from binary access control to a more sophisticated model of information governance, positing that the core challenge is not merely about permission, but about governing the flow of information. We introduce Agent Access Control (AAC), a novel framework that reframes AC as a dynamic, context-aware process of information flow governance.\nL18: AAC operates on two core modules: (1) multi-dimensional contextual evaluation, which assesses not just identity but also relationships, scenarios, and norms; and (2) adaptive response formulation, which moves beyond simple allow/deny decisions to shape information through redaction, summarization, and paraphrasing.\nL19: This vision, powered by a dedicated AC reasoning engine, aims to bridge the gap between human-like nuanced judgment and scalable Al safety, proposing a new conceptual lens for future research in trustworthy agent design.\nL20: Comments:  | 10 pages, 1 figure\nL21: Subjects:  | Multiagent Systems (cs.MA); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2510.11108 [cs.MA]\nL23:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2510.11108v1 [cs.MA] for this version)\nL24:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.11108†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Xinfeng Li [cite19†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Mon, 13 Oct 2025 07:57:09 UTC (154 KB)\nL29: cite5†[v2] Sun, 19 Oct 2025 04:48:38 UTC (145 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled A Vision for Access Control in LLM-based Agent Systems, by Xinfeng Li and 7 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite14†View PDF L38:   * cite15†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite20†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite21†view license†creativecommons.org L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.MA\nL45: \nL46: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L47: \nL48: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2025-10 L49: \nL50: 