e?†replicate.com )\nL125: \nL126: [Input] Spaces Toggle\nL127: \nL128: Hugging Face Spaces (cite44†What is Spaces?†huggingface.co )\nL129: \nL130: [Input] Spaces Toggle\nL131: \nL132: TXYZ.AI (cite45†What is TXYZ.AI?†txyz.ai )\nL133: \nL134: [Input] Related Papers\nL135: # Recommenders and Search Tools\nL136: \nL137: [Input] Link to Influence Flower\nL138: \nL139: Influence Flower (cite46†What are Influence Flowers?†influencemap.cmlab.dev )\nL140: \nL141: [Input] Core recommender toggle\nL142: \nL143: CORE Recommender (cite47†What is CORE?†core.ac.uk )\nL144: \nL145:   * Author\nL146:   * Venue\nL147:   * Institution\nL148:   * Topic\nL149: \nL150: [Input] About arXivLabs\nL151: # arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\nL152: \nL153: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL154: \nL155: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.10185v1] MedAgentAudit: Diagnosing and Quantifying Collaborative Failure Modes in Medical Multi-Agent Systems (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.10185v1)\nciteturn2741view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.10185v1\",\"lineno\":14}); Total lines: 165\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 11 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 27 May 2026 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:MedAgentAudit: Diagnosing and Quantifying Collaborative Failure Modes in Medical Multi-Agent Systems\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Lei Gu , cite7†Yinghao Zhu , cite8†Haoran Sang , cite9†Zixiang Wang , cite10†Dehao Sui , cite11†Wen Tang , cite12†Ewen Harrison , cite13†Junyi Gao , cite14†Lequan Yu , cite15†Liantao Ma L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled MedAgentAudit: Diagnosing and Quantifying Collaborative Failure Modes in Medical Multi-Agent Systems, by Lei Gu and 9 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite16†View PDF cite17†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:While large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems show promise in simulating medical consultations, their evaluation is often confined to final-answer accuracy.\nL17: This practice treats their internal collaborative processes as opaque \"black boxes\" and overlooks a critical question: is a diagnostic conclusion reached through a sound and verifiable reasoning pathway? The inscrutable nature of these systems poses a significant risk in high-stakes medical applications, potentially leading to flawed or untrustworthy conclusions. To address this, we conduct a large-scale empirical study of 3,600 cases from six medical datasets and six representative multi-agent frameworks.\nL18: Through a rigorous, mixed-methods approach combining qualitative analysis with quantitative auditing, we develop a comprehensive taxonomy of collaborative failure modes. Our quantitative audit reveals four dominant failure patterns: flawed consensus driven by shared model deficiencies, suppression of correct minority opinions, ineffective discussion dynamics, and critical information loss during synthesis.\nL19: This study demonstrates that high accuracy alone is an insufficient measure of clinical or public trust. It highlights the urgent need for transparent and auditable reasoning processes, a cornerstone for the responsible development and deployment of medical AI.\nL20: Comments:  | Code: cite18†this https URL†github.com L21: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite19†arXiv:2510.10185 [cs.CL]\nL23:    | (or cite20†arXiv:2510.10185v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL24:    | cite21†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.10185†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Yinghao Zhu [cite22†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Sat, 11 Oct 2025 11:48:57 UTC (837 KB)\nL29: cite23†[v2] Sat, 23 May 2026 13:52:37 UTC (7,542 KB)\nL30: cite5†[v3] Wed, 27 May 2026 14:30:07 UTC (7,542 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled MedAgentAudit: Diagnosing and Quantifying Collaborative Failure Modes in Medical Multi-Agent Systems, by Lei Gu and 9 other authors\nL37: \nL38:   * cite16†View PDF L39:   * cite17†HTML (experimental) L40:   * cite24†TeX Source L41: \nL42: cite25†view license L43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: cs.CL\nL46: \nL47: cite26†< prev |   cite27†next > L48: \nL49: cite28†new | cite29†recent | cite30†2025-10 L50: \nL51: Change to browse by:\nL52: \nL53: cite31†cs L54: cite32†cs.AI L55: cite33†cs.MA L56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * cite34†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L60:   * cite35†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L61:   * cite36†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L62: \nL63: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL64: \nL65: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL66: \nL67: [Button: ×]\nL68: \nL69: loading...\nL70: \nL71: Data provided by:\nL72: \nL73: ### Bookmark\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL76: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Bibliographic Explorer (cite37†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Connected Papers (cite38†What is Connected Papers?†www.connec